.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dkim2d
is the HTTP/JSON service around the standalone DKIM2 library.
Mail adapters such as
.BR dkim2\-milter (8),
.BR dkim2\-exim (8),
and
.BR dkim2\-dsn\-propagator (8)
send it complete messages; it verifies inbound DKIM2 chains, signs mail for
local domains, adds a revision hop to forwarded mail, and signs delivery
status notifications.
The daemon owns every protocol rule; the adapters only translate MTA
transactions into requests.
.PP
Each instance serves exactly the routes its configuration enables and
authenticates every caller with a per\-route capability file.
Configuration is one strict YAML document; protected material such as private
keys, capabilities, and replay keys is referenced by absolute path and is never
accepted on the command line or in environment values.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Load and validate the configuration, acquire every selected dependency, and
serve HTTP until SIGTERM or SIGINT.
Readiness is published only after every owner is live; shutdown withdraws
readiness first and then drains.
.TP
.B validate
Perform the same protected load as
.B serve
without opening a listener.
It is silent on success and never creates or rewrites protected state.
Run it after every configuration change.
.TP
.B probe
Send one two\-second, non\-proxied
.B GET /readyz
to the local daemon and succeed only on status 200.
It is intended for container and service\-manager health checks.
.TP
.B datasource bootstrap\-opendkim
Inventory legacy OpenDKIM key data and, with
.BR \-\-apply ,
publish it as one fenced datasource generation.
Dry\-run is the default.
.TP
.B datasource rollback
Republish prior datasource content under a higher generation.
.TP
.B datasource domain plan|prepare|dns export|prove|activate|status|reconcile|abort
Onboard one native signing domain offline through a protected operation
journal: plan the change, prepare keys, export the DNS records to publish,
prove them through a recursive resolver, and activate only with
.BR \-\-apply .
.TP
.B datasource rotation run|emergency|status|reconcile|abort|purge plan|purge apply
Run global key rotation campaigns offline through a protected campaign journal.
.B run
plans by default and executes only with
.BR \-\-apply ;
.B purge apply
destroys only the generations named in an exact, previously written plan.
.TP
.B datasource key generate
Create one owner\-only PKCS#8 private key file for flat\-file key custody and
print its public credential and the DNS TXT record to publish.
.SH OPTIONS
These options apply to
.BR serve ;
the overrides take precedence over YAML and environment values.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIstring\fR
absolute path to the configuration document
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
help for dkim2d
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
version for dkim2d
.TP
\fB\-\-listen\fR \fIstring\fR
override server.listen
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\-mode\fR \fIstring\fR
override policy.mode
.TP
\fB\-\-replay\-backend\fR \fIstring\fR
override replay.backend
.SS Probe options
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIport\fR
Local readiness port (default 8080).
.TP
\fB\-\-connect\-address\fR \fIip\fR
Exact local listener IP to dial (default 127.0.0.1).
Without TLS it must be a loopback address.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-server\-name\fR \fIname\fR
Verify the private\-network TLS server identity; requires
.B \-\-tls\-ca\-file
and a private
.BR \-\-connect\-address .
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-ca\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
Absolute path of the internal PKI trust roots.
.SS Datasource options
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Validate and report without writing (the default for
.B bootstrap\-opendkim
and
.BR "rotation run" ).
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Authorize the one exact mutation the command describes.
Activation, emergency rotation, abort, and purge refuse to run without it.
.TP
.B \-\-machine
Emit one deterministic, bounded JSON document instead of the human report.
.TP
\fB\-\-algorithm\fR \fIalgorithm\fR
Key algorithm for
.BR "key generate" :
.BR rsa\-sha256 ,
.BR ed25519\-sha256 ,
or
.BR mldsa44 .
.TP
\fB\-\-rsa\-bits\fR \fIbits\fR
RSA modulus size: 2048, 3072, or 4096.
.TP
\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fIseconds\fR
TTL of the printed DNS record.
.PP
The remaining datasource options name absolute protected files (configuration,
intent, operation or campaign journal, plan, and output artifact) or exact
binding values, as shown in the synopsis.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every stable configuration path except
.B config.version
and
.B protected.generation
has one explicit
.B DKIM2D_
environment name, for example
.BR DKIM2D_SERVER_LISTEN .
Other names are ignored.
Scalar YAML values may contain one non\-recursive
.B ${NAME}
expansion; a missing variable is a configuration error.
Precedence is defaults, YAML, environment, then command\-line overrides.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, help output, or a clean shutdown.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, startup, dependency, serve, shutdown, or probe failure, or a
runtime without the mandatory
.B mldsa44
algorithm.
Diagnostics are fixed and content\-free.
.TP
.B 2
Command shape or flag error, including a missing
.B \-\-apply
where it is required.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dkim2/dkim2d\-*.yaml
Configuration documents used by the administrator guides, one per instance
(for example inbound and signer).
.TP
.I /var/lib/dkim2/<instance>/<generation>/
Protected generation directory (mode 0500) named by
.BR protected.generation ,
holding capabilities, replay keys, and certificates, as laid out in the
guides.
It is immutable after publication; create a new generation, point the YAML
file at it, and restart.
There is no hot reload.
.SH SECURITY
Run one instance per trust role.
Keep the HTTP listener on loopback or a private network with TLS; the daemon
rejects proxies, redirects, and ambient credentials.
Secret bytes never appear in logs, metrics, traces, or command output.
